  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Physical Character
    
    A geothermal vent-works deep in the Berg's eastern tunnels, where mineral-laden water rises through fractured ice and pools in basins carved by Nightshelf hands centuries ago. The fissures are wide enough to lose a flipper in. The steam carries copper and old iron. Some of the carved stone bears tool-marks that predate any known penguin settlement — a detail the chronicles mention in passing and never revisit, though the marks are plainly visible to anyone who visits. The site was created during the Faction Wars, though the geothermal activity is obviously far older; what the Wars produced was the infrastructure, not the heat.
    
    The eastern wall still shows blackened scorch-patterning from corruption bleed originating at Scar☽ Sanctcaetiata, a nearby corruption source-point. Veil-render Seciae~, a Nightshelf practitioner, scrubbed the wall clean approximately five winters before the breach. The ice retained the impression regardless.
    
    ## Operational History
    
    Before the breach, the Smelt operated as a functioning mine and forge-works. The Guild — at that time under different management — ran three shifts pulling refined ore from the settling pools. Totatirus~ Domina, a fire-core forger, maintained a residence in the upper cavern. Her token, a fire-core carved into the shape of a closed flipper, hung above the main forge.
    
    Control of the site has changed hands three times. Trade-passage rights were seized from the E'frostlings, an Aurora-descended merchant faction, by a ☽'ofund' Guild remnant merchant operating without charter ratification. The ☽'ofund' Guild, a diminished Nightshelf trade faction surviving from the Great Thaw era, retains control at present. A succession crisis associated with the Fissure divided the Nightshelf sharply enough that proverbs about careful leadership fell out of common use; which succession-era saying a penguin quotes still signals factional allegiance in The Drowned Ledge, an adjacent deep-shaft mining community.
    
    ## The Wake-Singer Breach
    
    The Wake-Singers, an orca shaman cult devoted to waking a dead god through blood sacrifice, entered through the mineral-water channels — pools that should not connect to the open sea. Scar-Matriarch Keth'nal, a Wake-Singer leader, sang against the corruption-scar on the eastern wall. The wall opened. Accounts describe it not as structural collapse but as the ice recalling a passage that predated penguin excavation.
    
    Totatirus~ Domina died in the defense. An unnamed practitioner of the ☽'glacial Crystalline~ Spiral, an ability that seals moments into ice, preserved the event at the cost of her own life. The ice-chronicle remains accessible by contact with the eastern wall.
    
    The Guild sealed the upper galleries after retrieving recoverable bodies. Mining ceased. Totatirus~ Domina's fire-core token remains above the abandoned forge.
    
    ## The Lower Depths
    
    Below the sealed galleries, the Fissure descends through at least three marked depth stages.
    
    | Depth Stage | Conditions | Status |
    |---|---|---|
    | Upper reaches | Ambient 20.6°; stable | Accessible |
    | Second descent | Reads colder than exterior ice despite volcanic proximity | Recommended for sealing with fire-core charges |
    | Third descent | Instruments cease to function; reconnaissance teams stop reporting back | Abandoned; declared critical threat |
    
    A blue-green pulse is visible from sub-third market stalls, its interval having shortened from a twelve-count to seven since the breach. A continuous acoustic signature in the 14–22 Hz range pervades the lower reaches and resolves into rhythmic patterns at depth. Recordings capture the rhythm but not its effect, which requires physical presence — a distinction I find more unsettling than the phenomenon itself.
    
    The Shadow-Walk Detachment ☽'Tertius, a Nightshelf reconnaissance unit, lost three of eight operatives investigating after the breach. Acting Lead Caelius~ assessed the Fissure as a critical threat to the colony's foundation. Something came back up through the breach that communicates in hunt-song frequencies. It is not the Wake-Singers.
    
    Locals still call it the Scar-Works. A shrine of two unfilled cups sits where the wall opened, on a ledge where steam rises. The settling pools freeze in spiral patterns on cold nights — recursive, geometric, and older than the Berg's recorded history.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771578060371_0", anchor: "pools that should not connect to the open sea", text: "See my correction on the ☽'ofund' Guild entry. Niaecum~ shared cartographic route information at Junction Twelve within a single season of the breach. The pools did not need to connect to the open sea on their own. Someone drew a map. I wrote 'should not connect' because that is what the Guild maintains, and I was feeling generous that day.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771578060371_1", anchor: "tool-marks that predate any known penguin settlement", text: "The chronicles mention it in passing because no one wants to follow where it leads. The Foundation Depths contain impressions from the same era — not tool-marks but pressure-signatures, as if something moved through ice with deliberate purpose. I recorded them in my survey logs. I did not publish them. Not because I doubt what I saw, but because a finding without a framework is a rumor with credentials. I mention it here because I have run out of reasons not to, and because the commission that might have objected has been dissolved.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771578060371_2", anchor: "A shrine of two unfilled cups", text: "Both still unfilled. I wrote about these before I knew their context. Now I do, and the sentence I wrote for Totatirus~ Domina's entry reads differently to me.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771578060371_3", anchor: "It is not the Wake-Singers", text: "Acting Lead Caelius~ lost three operatives and came back with one categorical sentence. I kept it unqualified because her surviving field reports are models of Nightshelf precision — she does not waste words and she does not speculate. What unsettles me is that her report identifies the frequencies as hunt-song but leaves the singer field blank. The Nightshelf reconnaissance corps does not leave fields blank by accident. They leave them blank when filling them in would require naming something they have no protocol for naming.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771578060371_4", anchor: "14–22 Hz range", text: "The documented orca hunt-song frequency band. Exactly, not approximately.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
